    "created_at": "2026-10-08T14:30:13Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-08T14:31:48Z",
    "body": "My impression for the longest time was that part of why we had the very confusing term \"non-subresource\" in the ServiceWorkers spec[1] was to explicitly allow treating notification image loads as something that should be intercepted by the Service Worker not as a subresource request but instead like a navigation, making it step 9 of [handle fetch](https://w3c.github.io/ServiceWorker/#handle-fetch) instead of step 10.  It seems like this commit may be the first to explicitly encode the fetch destination type as \"image\".  Is there a specific rationale for \"image\" / did we consider the possibility of giving it its own destination type?\r\n\r\nThe motivating issue is https://github.com/w3c/ServiceWorker/issues/1850 where we do need to address the active worker concept for both step 9 and step 10 but I think some of the original concerns in https://github.com/whatwg/fetch/issues/303 about how to handle notification images did stem from the awkwardness of the ServiceWorker conceptually intercepting its own fetches, which is what the image fetch being a subresource implies.  Whereas if the request was conceptually more top-level, it's slightly less awkward?\r\n\r\nNB: I do realize https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#subresource-request and https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#non-subresource-request are in fetch.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
